Let $M$ be a fixed bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^2$ (or, more generally, a fixed Riemannian manifold with boundary) whose boundary $\partial M = \Gamma_{\rm in} \sqcup \Gamma_{\rm out} \sqcup \Gamma_{\rm wall}$ is partitioned into a "source" part $\Gamma_{\rm in}$, a "sink" part $\Gamma_{\rm out}$, and an impermeable part $\Gamma_{\rm wall}$. Consider the motion of an ideal incompressible fluid in $M$ governed by the Euler equations
$$\partial_t v + (v\cdot\nabla)v = -\nabla p, \qquad \mathrm{div}\, v = 0,$$
with the following data prescribed in addition to the initial velocity $v(0)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item the normal component $v\cdot n$ of the velocity on the penetrable boundary $\Gamma_{\rm in}\cup\Gamma_{\rm out}$ (with $v\cdot n<0$ on $\Gamma_{\rm in}$ and $v\cdot n>0$ on $\Gamma_{\rm out}$);
\item the vorticity $\omega = \mathrm{curl}\, v$ specified on the source part $\Gamma_{\rm in}$, transported into $M$ by the flow;
\item possibly an exterior forcing field $f$ acting on the fluid.
\end{itemize}
Because mass and energy can be supplied or drained through the boundary, the system is not energy-conserving in general.

\textbf{Problem.} Construct a variational principle — preferably a geodesic formulation on an appropriate infinite-dimensional configuration space (such as a Lie group, Lie groupoid, or Lie algebroid of partially defined volume-preserving diffeomorphisms of $M$) — whose stationary points (resp. geodesics with respect to a suitable kinetic-energy-type metric) are exactly the solutions of this Euler-type system with sources and sinks. The formulation must specify:
\begin{enumerate}
\item how the prescribed boundary flux $v\cdot n$ on $\Gamma_{\rm in}\cup\Gamma_{\rm out}$ enters the action;
\item how the transported boundary vorticity data on $\Gamma_{\rm in}$ enters the action;
\item whether and how exterior forces $f$ should be incorporated;
\item whether and how a "memory" of the fluid (recording the history of particles that have entered through $\Gamma_{\rm in}$ and have not yet exited through $\Gamma_{\rm out}$) must be incorporated.
\end{enumerate}
A model case is the horizontal pipe in which fluid enters at one end and exits at the other.

